  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\n...Synopsis... \n\nLow pressure across the western Dakotas will slowly propagate \neast/northeast over the next day or so, along with showers ahead of \nthis system. As the system evolves the upper ridge will flatten \nbecoming more zonal and eventually northwest flow aloft through the \nperiod. Confidence is relatively high for this large scale pattern \nto evolve given general agreement among ensemble \nmembers/systems. Temperatures through the period will be near or\neven slightly below normal. \n\n..Showers/Storms into Saturday... \n\nThe strongest forcing and heaviest (most widespread) rainfall will \nbe along and north of the surface low track. Generally speaking, the \ntrack will be west to east along the International border. Although \nscenarios are not too widespread, there still remains minor \ndifferences from ensemble member to ensemble member regarding the \ntrack of the surface low, and a bit further south track would \nbring an inch or two of rainfall to the northern parts of our \nregion. Instability very weak to nill, and do not expect much \nfor impacts other than whatever impacts a wetting rain may \nbring. To the south of the surface low track coverage will be \nmore spotty. \n\n...Severe Potential Early Next Week...\n\nAs another stronger wave propagates through Canada greater \ninstability will advect into the region, ahead of a cold front. \nMultiple scenarios exist regarding how this will evolve, including \nstrength and timing differences, which will ultimately decide on \nstorm coverage and intensity - severe potential. ML/AI guidance \nindicate a signal for severe storms Monday-Tuesday.",
